---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="WHOlives | Community Transformation Trust" description="CT Trust supports WHOlives, creating sustainable access to clean water through interest-free community loans and the portable Village Drill.">
  <div class="report-banner-wrap">
    <img class="report-banner" src="/images/projects/wholives.png" alt="" loading="eager" />
  </div>
  <article class="wrap report-article">
    <h1>WHOlives</h1>

    <section class="reveal">
      <h3>Creating Sustainable Access to Clean Water Through Community Loans</h3>
      <p>Community Transformation Trust is proud to support the work of WHOlives, a non-profit dedicated to helping vulnerable communities gain access to clean water through interest-free loans. Recently, we contributed to their efforts by purchasing a Village Drill &mdash; an innovative, portable borehole drill that dramatically lowers the cost of well installation.</p>
      <p>You can help support WHOlives projects by making a donation via our <a href="/donation">Donations Page</a>. Donations made through our charity are tax-deductible in New Zealand.</p>
      <img class="report-photo" src="/images/projects/wholives-1.png" alt="The portable Village Drill purchased for WHOlives" loading="lazy" />
    </section>

    <section class="reveal">
      <h3>A Smarter Approach to Clean Water</h3>
      <p>Rather than giving away wells for free (a model that often leads to broken pumps and no long-term maintenance), WHOlives provides interest-free loans to poor communities so they can pay for a borehole to be drilled in their village. Once the loan is repaid, the funds are re-loaned to another community &mdash; creating a self-sustaining cycle of clean water access.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="reveal">
      <h3>How It Works</h3>
      <p>WHOlives focuses on some of the world&rsquo;s most impoverished regions, currently prioritizing rural Kenya and Uganda. Trained local teams use the Village Drill to install wells in villages. Families contribute a portion of the cost, encouraging ownership from day one.</p>
      <p>In addition to drilling the boreholes, WHOlives provides training to ensure each village can maintain its water source long-term. The model creates local accountability and economic incentive &mdash; key to keeping the wells operational for generations.</p>
      <img class="report-photo" src="/images/projects/wholives-2.png" alt="A trained local team installing a well with the Village Drill" loading="lazy" />
    </section>

    <section class="reveal">
      <h3>Long-Term Impact</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Encourages local ownership and sustainability</li>
        <li>Creates jobs through trained, low-cost drilling teams</li>
        <li>Improves health, hygiene, and education (especially for girls)</li>
        <li>Reduces dependency by promoting community-led solutions</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="reveal">
      <h3>See Current Projects &amp; Make a Difference</h3>
      <p>You can view WHOlives&rsquo; active projects on their <a href="https://wholives.org/our-projects/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Our Projects</a> page.</p>
      <p>To support these active projects you can donate via our Donations Page (donations made via our Donations Page will be allocated by us to the WHOlives Projects, and are tax-deductible in New Zealand as our charity the Community Transformation Trust has overseas donee status).</p>
      <p><a href="/donation" class="btn-donate">Make a donation</a></p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
